e": "## Analysis\n\n**Change point located.** Neighborhood turnover for the group peaks at exactly 1.0 (complete contact replacement) at t\u224853324, sustained across 53324\u201355889, then falls back to ~0.5 baseline (step_02).\n\n**The switch is a clean partition, not drift.** With half-windows of 5000 on each side of 53324, the group's contact edge sets have Jaccard = 0.0: 137 edges before, 81 after, zero overlap (step_04). Two disjoint cohorts appear: old = {18, 339, 566, 633, 702, 706, 719, 976, 1082, 1084, 1466, 1485, 1595, 1710}, new = {61, 189, 212, 232, 309, 381, 647, 684, 698, 739, 768, 1303, 1810, 1872, 1964}.\n\n**Cohort volumes flip across the boundary** (steps 10, 11): the old cohort carries 530 events before vs 23 after; the new cohort 14 before vs 557 after. Each cohort is active essentially only on its own side.\n\n**Confounder controls:**\n- *Volume*: activity is flat (~73\u201395 events/bin) on both sides \u2014 no burst driving the signal. Only the transition bin itself is quiet (2 events, step_03), a brief structural gap, so the switch is not a volume artifact.\n- *Gradual background turnover*: matched non-transition window pairs for the same group give Jaccard 0.47 (step_08) and 0.42 (step_09) \u2014 the group normally retains ~45% of partners. Retention collapsing to 0.0 only at 53324 is a genuine anomaly, not this group's ordinary churn rate.\n- *Sparse-graph baseline*: the whole graph has low edge overlap everywhere (0.043 in a control pair, step_13), so global Jaccard is uninformative \u2014 but the group's *own* retention baseline (~0.45) is what breaks, which isolates the effect to these nodes.\n- *Static connectivity*: node 653 is time-ignorant \"connected\" to both an old partner (566) and a new one (212), 3 events each (steps 14, 15) \u2014 proving the distinction is purely temporal, not a matter of edge existence.\n- *Narrow transition*: at bandwidth 2000 (step_12), turnover is 0.07 just before, spikes to 1.0 across 53607\u201355607, and returns to ~0.12\u20130.25 after \u2014 confirming an abrupt hand-off rather than a slow ramp.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_60b67dfe\",\n  \"decision\": \"present\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [431, 596, 653, 759, 807, 900, 923, 930, 1064, 1159, 1663, 1783],\n  \"window\": [39607, 69607],\n  \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_10\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_12\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_09\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_13\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_15\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"The 12-node group switches its entire contact cohort at t\u224853324. Half-windows of 5000 units on each side share zero edges (Jaccard 0.0; 137 old vs 81 new), and turnover peaks at 1.0 there while sitting near 0.45 retention in matched control window pairs for the same group. The old cohort (18, 339, 566, 633, 702, 706, 719, 976, 1082, 1084, 1466, 1485, 1595, 1710) drops from 530 to 23 events while the new cohort (61, 189, 212, 232, 309, 381, 647, 684, 698, 739, 768, 1303, 1810, 1872, 1964) rises from 14 to 557. Event volume is flat across the period, so this is not a burst; the graph-wide low edge overlap is a sparsity baseline that cannot explain the group's own retention collapsing to zero; and static connectivity to both cohorts exists, so the effect is purely temporal. The transition is confined to a short interval (~53324-55600), indicating an abrupt regime switch rather than gradual drift.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 53990, "total_output_tokens": 5813, "total_tokens": 59803, "total_latency_ms": 80853, "llm_calls": 6, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}